<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import idPageSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/[id].abide' with { type: 'text' }
import errorPageSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/error.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import exportCompaniesSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/exportCompanies.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import getCompanySource from 'crm-06/src/companies/getCompany.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import indexPageSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/index.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import layoutSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/layout.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import CompaniesFrame from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/CompaniesFrame.abide'
import companiesFrameSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/CompaniesFrame.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import CompanyPages from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/CompanyPages.abide'
import companyPagesSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/CompanyPages.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import companySheetSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/CompanySheet.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import CompanyTabs from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/CompanyTabs.abide'
import companyTabsSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/CompanyTabs.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import ExportButton from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/ExportButton.abide'
import exportButtonSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/ExportButton.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import MissingCompany from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/MissingCompany.abide'
import missingCompanySource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/MissingCompany.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import SheetPreview from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/SheetPreview.abide'
import sheetPreviewSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/SheetPreview.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import TransitionFrame from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/TransitionFrame.abide'
import transitionFrameSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/TransitionFrame.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import SlowCompany from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/SlowCompany.abide'
import slowCompanySource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/SlowCompany.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import TabTitle from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/TabTitle.abide'
import tabTitleSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/TabTitle.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import previewCompanySource from 'crm-06/src/companies/previewCompany.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import companyPageTestSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/CompanyPage.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import companyTabsTestSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/CompanyTabs.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import errorPageTestSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/ErrorPage.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import exportButtonTestSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/ExportButton.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import headTestSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/Head.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import layoutTestSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/Layout.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import sheetPreviewTestSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/lib/SheetPreview.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import streamingTestSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/Streaming.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import viewTransitionTestSource from 'crm-06/src/companies/ViewTransition.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A file's path is its address">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Answers on</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>src/companies/index.abide</code></td><td><code>/companies</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/companies/[id].abide</code></td><td><code>/companies/hopper-and-co</code>, and every other id</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/companies/layout.abide</code></td><td>no address: it wraps every page at <code>/companies</code> and below</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/companies/error.abide</code></td><td>no address: it answers when a page below it fails</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/companies/getCompany.ts</code></td><td><code>GET /companies/getCompany</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>An <code>**/index.abide</code> answers its directory's own address, and any other
        <code>**/&lt;name&gt;.abide</code> adds its name. A part of the path in brackets is a value
        the page reads:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Named</th><th>Matches</th><th>In <code>route.params</code></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>[name]</code></td><td>one segment, required</td><td>text</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>[[name]]</code></td><td>one segment or none</td><td>text, absent when nothing matched</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>[...name]</code></td><td>every remaining segment</td><td>a list, empty when nothing matched</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`[id].abide` answers for every company">
        <Example
            id="crm-06-company-page"
            app="crm-06"
            title="`[id].abide` — one file for every company"
            summary="Two companies, one page file. Pick one: the address changes, and the page loads the company the address names."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/CompanyPages.abide': companyPagesSource,
                'src/companies/[id].abide': idPageSource,
                'src/companies/CompanyPage.test.ts': companyPageTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <CompanyPages />
        </Example>

        <p><code>route.params.id</code> is the segment the address matched. It is a reactive value
        like any other, so moving from one company to the next reloads the company rather than
        rebuilding the page.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the CRM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>route.name</code></td><td>the pattern that matched, <code>/companies/[id]</code> for every company, so a log line keys on the page rather than on each address</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Params</code></td><td>what the segments matched; a query string is never in it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>RouteParams&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>the type <code>route.params</code> has in a page or layout filed under <code>P</code>, so <code>id</code> is text and a key the route lacks is an error. A component under <code>lib/</code> has no route of its own and reads the loose <code>Params</code>: pass it the value as a prop, or narrow it with <code>RouteParams&lt;'/…'&gt;</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A layout holds what the companies pages share">
        <Example
            id="crm-06-layout"
            app="crm-06"
            title="`layout.abide` — the header the companies pages share, built once"
            summary="The companies header holds a note for the call you are on. Type one, then open a company and come back: the page changes, and the note stays."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/CompaniesFrame.abide': companiesFrameSource,
                'src/companies/layout.abide': layoutSource,
                'src/companies/Layout.test.ts': layoutTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <CompaniesFrame />
        </Example>

        <p>A <code>**/layout.abide</code> wraps every page at its directory and below, and
        <code>&lt;slot/&gt;</code> is where the page renders. A navigation between two of its pages
        swaps what is in the slot and leaves the layout alone, so its inputs, its scroll and
        anything it was loading stay as they were. Layouts nest: the root layout wraps this one.</p>

        <p>A layout's <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> files hooks for the same pages. The
        companies layout files its <code>onError</code> hook there.</p>

        <p>A package can ship pages too. <code>import '…' with &#123; type: 'routes' &#125;</code>
        in a file of your app serves every page and handler in the named directory below that
        file's directory, and moving the line moves them.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A link names a route, and `url` fills it in">
        <p>The list links each company with
        <code>url('/companies/[id]', &#123; id: company.id &#125;)</code>. You write the pattern a
        page is filed under rather than the address, and <code>url</code> fills in the segments.
        Leaving out a required one is a compile error, and a pattern no page is filed under is
        refused by <code>abide check</code>. A query goes last:
        <code>url('/companies', &#123;&#125;, &#123; city: 'London' &#125;)</code>.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the CRM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>Routes</code></td><td>every pattern the CRM files a page under, declared by <code>abide check</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>RouteLiteral</code></td><td>one of those patterns: what <code>url</code> takes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Query</code></td><td>the query argument, a record or a <code>URLSearchParams</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ParamsOf&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>the params object pattern <code>P</code> takes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>HasParams&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>whether <code>P</code> requires one</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>HasSegments&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>whether <code>P</code> has any bracketed segment</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>RequiredNames&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>its <code>[name]</code> segments</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OptionalNames&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>its <code>[[name]]</code> segments</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>RestNames&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>its <code>[...name]</code> segments</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>You never write the last six. They are how <code>url</code>'s signature is read off the
        pattern, and a compile error naming <code>RequiredNames</code> is naming the segment you
        left out.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`navigate` moves the address from code">
        <Example
            id="crm-06-navigate"
            app="crm-06"
            title="`navigate` — a new address, the same page"
            summary="A company page keeps its open tab in the address, so a link can open it. Type a draft, then switch tabs: the address moves, and the draft stays."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/CompanyTabs.abide': companyTabsSource,
                'src/companies/lib/CompanyTabs.test.ts': companyTabsTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <CompanyTabs />
        </Example>

        <p>An address the page already shown matches moves <code>route.url</code> and keeps the
        page mounted. Another page's address mounts that page in place, without reloading the
        document. <code>replace: true</code> writes over the history entry, so Back leaves the
        company rather than stepping through every tab.</p>

        <p>A plain <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> click does the same. Reach for
        <code>navigate</code> when code decides where to go. <code>route.navigating</code> is
        true while a navigation is in flight, which is what a progress bar reads.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A view transition carries the company across pages">
        <Example
            id="crm-06-view-transition"
            app="crm-06"
            title="`view-transition-name` — a company name that moves to where it lands"
            summary="The list row and the company page give the company's name the same view-transition-name. Open a company: the name travels from its row to the heading."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/TransitionFrame.abide': transitionFrameSource,
                'src/companies/index.abide': indexPageSource,
                'src/companies/[id].abide': idPageSource,
                'src/companies/ViewTransition.test.ts': viewTransitionTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <TransitionFrame />
        </Example>

        <p>Elements on two pages that share a <code>view-transition-name</code> are animated from
        one place to the other. A <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> rule naming a view transition turns
        them on. The build reads it, so an app with none never looks for one at run time. A
        navigation waits for the DOM to be written, not for the animation. A browser without view
        transitions navigates as it otherwise would.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`<head>` in a page replaces its layout's">
        <Example
            id="crm-06-head"
            app="crm-06"
            title="`<head>` in a page and its layout — the deepest title wins"
            summary="The companies layout titles every page, and a company page titles itself. Open the list, then a company: the tab takes the company's name."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/TabTitle.abide': tabTitleSource,
                'src/companies/layout.abide': layoutSource,
                'src/companies/[id].abide': idPageSource,
                'src/companies/Head.test.ts': headTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <TabTitle />
        </Example>

        <p>A <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> sits at the top level of a file, never inside a block. A
        <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, a <code>&lt;meta&gt;</code> by its name and a
        <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> by its <code>rel</code> and <code>href</code> each have a key,
        and the deepest contribution for a key wins. One with no key is appended. Leaving a page puts its layout's
        title back.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The page goes out before the company lands">
        <Example
            id="crm-06-streaming"
            app="crm-06"
            title="A hole per value in flight — the page first, the company after"
            summary="The company database takes over a second. Load the page again: the labels arrive at once, and each value fills its own hole when the company lands."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/SlowCompany.abide': slowCompanySource,
                'src/companies/[id].abide': idPageSource,
                'src/companies/getCompany.ts': getCompanySource,
                'src/companies/Streaming.test.ts': streamingTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <SlowCompany />
        </Example>

        <p>Nothing holds the document for a value. The page gets one turn to start its loads, and
        a value that answers in that turn is painted. A value still in flight leaves a hole, and
        the browser's own call fills it, so the document carries no copy of the answer.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The hole</th><th>What fills</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>&#123;text&#125;</code> in markup</td><td>the text</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>href=&#123;x&#125;</code>, any attribute</td><td>the attribute, on the element itself</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>class:</code>, <code>style:</code>, <code>bind:</code></td><td>the class, the style property, the bound property</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;title&gt;&#123;name&#125;&lt;/title&gt;</code></td><td>the element's <code>textContent</code>, so the tab title fills too</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;textarea&gt;&#123;v&#125;&lt;/textarea&gt;</code></td><td>its <code>defaultValue</code>, so a reader's typing is kept</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`error.abide` answers when a page cannot">
        <Example
            id="crm-06-error-page"
            app="crm-06"
            title="`error.abide` — a company that is not there"
            summary="An old link points at a company that was merged away. The handler refuses at once, and the companies error page answers with a 404."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/MissingCompany.abide': missingCompanySource,
                'src/companies/error.abide': errorPageSource,
                'src/companies/getCompany.ts': getCompanySource,
                'src/companies/ErrorPage.test.ts': errorPageTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <MissingCompany />
        </Example>

        <p>An <code>**/error.abide</code> is found the way a layout is: the nearest one above the
        page that failed. It gets the refusal as <code>failure</code>, and each refusal's
        <code>is</code> narrows it. An address no page answers is <code>notFound</code> too. A
        page asked with a method other than <code>GET</code> or <code>HEAD</code> is a 405,
        painted by the same page, with <code>allow: GET, HEAD</code> on the answer.</p>

        <p>The refusal has to come in the page's first turn to set the status. That is why
        <code>getCompany</code> refuses before it waits. An error page that fails itself hands
        off to the next one up.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`onError` reports what the caller is not told">
        <Example
            id="crm-06-error-report"
            app="crm-06"
            title="`onError(fn)` — the tracker hears what the caller is not told"
            summary="The export worker has stopped answering. Run the export: the button gets a bare 500, and the hook in the companies layout gets the reason."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/ExportButton.abide': exportButtonSource,
                'src/companies/exportCompanies.ts': exportCompaniesSource,
                'src/companies/layout.abide': layoutSource,
                'src/companies/lib/ExportButton.test.ts': exportButtonTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ExportButton />
        </Example>

        <p><code>onError</code> runs on an unexpected error in any request below the file that
        called it. Send the error to a tracker there. The caller gets a 500 with nothing about the
        cause, so a stack trace never reaches a browser.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`render` builds a document from a component">
        <Example
            id="crm-06-render"
            app="crm-06"
            title="`render(component)` — an account sheet from the component itself"
            summary="A rep sends a customer a one-page account sheet. The handler renders the sheet component into a document of its own, and the frame shows it."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/SheetPreview.abide': sheetPreviewSource,
                'src/companies/previewCompany.ts': previewCompanySource,
                'src/companies/lib/CompanySheet.abide': companySheetSource,
                'src/companies/lib/SheetPreview.test.ts': sheetPreviewTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <SheetPreview />
        </Example>

        <p><code>CompanySheet(&#123; company &#125;)</code> is a <code>Component</code>: the
        component bound to its props, not yet rendered. <code>render</code> turns it into the
        document's bytes, built whole, and <code>page</code> answers with them as HTML.</p>

        <p>A component written inside <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> is the whole document, so the
        sheet carries its own styles and no bundle. The CRM's root layout writes its document the
        same way.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Serve the CRM under a sub-path">
        <Code code={`APP_URL=https://example.com/crm abide start`} />

        <p>Set <code>APP_URL</code> and every page, handler and asset answers below its path.
        Nothing is rebuilt: <code>url</code>, a handler's address and every asset reference are
        resolved when they are read, never baked into the build. An address you write out by hand
        is not, which is one more reason to build them with <code>url</code>.</p>

        <p>Everything abide serves for itself, from handler calls to the health check, is below
        <code>/__abide/**</code>, so a proxy in front of the CRM forwards one path family.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/guide/decide-who-sees-what')}>Who sees what</a> — sign a user in,
            and decide what each caller may read</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
